issue_context.body:
### Describe the bug
If `HubConnection.StopAsync` is called inside an `On` handler from the .NET client it will never return. 

During my testing, I've also noticed that if any other thread calls `StopAsync` from somewhere else during this period, it will also never return and the cancellation token will not fire, even if cancellation has been requested.

Edit: included the scenario described above.

### To Reproduce
#### Server
```cs
using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Builder;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;

namespace SignalRDeadlockReproduce
{
    public class FooHub : Hub
    {
        public FooHub(IHubContext<FooHub> hubContext)
        {
            var t = new Task(async () =>
            {
                await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
                await hubContext.Clients.All.SendAsync("deadlock");
            }, TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning);
            t.Start();
        }
    }

    public class Startup
    {
        public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services)
        {
            services.AddSignalR();
        }

        public void Configure(IApplicationBuilder app, IWebHostEnvironment env)
        {
            if (env.IsDevelopment())
            {
                app.UseDeveloperExceptionPage();
            }

            app.UseRouting();

            app.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
            {
                endpoints.MapHub<FooHub>("/foo");
            });
        }
    }
}
```

```xml
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>netcoreapp3.1</TargetFramework>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR" Version="1.1.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
```

#### Client
```cs
using System;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR.Client;
using static System.Console;

namespace SignalRDeadlockReproduce.Console
{
    class Program
    {
        static async Task Main(string[] args)
        {
            var conn = new HubConnectionBuilder().WithUrl("http://localhost:54841/foo").Build();
            conn.On("deadlock", async () =>
            {
                WriteLine("Here it comes");
                await conn.StopAsync();
                WriteLine("Done! Press any key to exit..."); // Will never print
            });

            await conn.StartAsync();
            WriteLine("Connection open...");

            await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));
            new Thread(async () =>
            {
                WriteLine("Me too!");
                var cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
                cts.CancelAfter(500);
                try
                {
                    await conn.StopAsync(cts.Token);
                }
                catch (Exception)
                {
                    WriteLine("Cancelled"); // Will never print
                }
                WriteLine("Yay! I also deadlocks :)"); // Will never print
            }).Start();

            ReadKey();
        }
    }
}

```

```xml
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>netcoreapp3.1</TargetFramework>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR.Client" Version="3.1.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
```

I use the background task to emulate the currently suggested workaround to closing connections server-side outside of hubs (which is calling a `KindlyCloseYourConnection` method on the client, since `IHubContext` doesn't offer a way to `Abort` connections). The same thing happens if the client `InvokeAsync` a method that eventually calls `deadlock` though.

